#[doc = r#"The enumeration of all capabilities that a Passive Optical sensor's FPA channel may support."#]
#[derive(Clone, Debug, Deserialize, PartialEq, Serialize)]
#[serde(rename = "PO_FPA_ChannelOperationEnum")]
pub enum PoFpaChannelOperationEnum {
	#[doc = r#"FPA channel's having this capability support the ability of modifying the range of frequencies of photons that they will collect and measure. This capability is rare since the channel's band is usually a result of the process used to manufacture the FPA and is therefore fixed."#]
	#[serde(rename = "BAND_RANGE")]
	BandRange,
	#[doc = r#"FPA channel's having this capability can change the way pixel values are computed. Normally the reported pixel value is directly related to the number of photons collected in one of the FPA's collection buckets. Some FPAs support the ability to aggregate buckets when generate the pixel value. For example, instead of using a single bucket, 2 or 4 buckets are used (linear arrays) or a 2x2 or a 4x4 set of buckets are used."#]
	#[serde(rename = "PIXEL_AGGREGATION")]
	PixelAggregation,
	#[doc = r#"FPA channel's having this capability can increase their sensitivity to the arriving photons by adding a "time" dimension to the FPA and then moving the collection buckets along that time dimension as the collection is performed. This basically increases the length of time photons are collected generally without increasing any smear in the image."#]
	#[serde(rename = "TIME_DELAY_INTEGRATION")]
	TimeDelayIntegration,
	#[doc = r#"FPA channel's having this capability support the ability of enhancing the value of the pixels it returns by specifying a "offset" that is applied to the measurement that increases the brightness of the pixel and a "gain" that is used to increase the channels sensitivity. For such channels, the resulting pixel value is computed as "offset + (gain * measuredValue)"."#]
	#[serde(rename = "PIXEL_SCALING")]
	PixelScaling,
	#[doc = r#"FPA channel's having this capability support the ability of the Focal Plane Arrays (FPA) to specify whether a zero pixel value represents "hot" or "cold". This capability is generally only available with FPAs that measure photons in the infrared frequencies."#]
	#[serde(rename = "PIXEL_POLARITY")]
	PixelPolarity,
	#[doc = r#"FPA channel's having this capability support the ability of modifying the length of time used by a channel to measure arriving photons. Increasing this value increases the channel's sensitivity but also increases the smear in the generated pixels. Setting this length of time is generally depending on how fast the sensor's line of sight (LOS) is moving across the surface being imaged."#]
	#[serde(rename = "COLLECTION_TIME")]
	CollectionTime,
	#[doc = r#"FPA channel's having this capability support the ability of correcting differences in sensitivity between the FPA's collection elements. Theoretically, each collection element within a FPA should have the same sensitivity as all other collection elements within the FPA. However due to limits in the manufacturing process, this is never the case. To correct this defect in the manufacturing process, FPAs may contain logic to adjust this "non-uniformity" such that resulting measurements appear as if they were made from a totally uniform FPA."#]
	#[serde(rename = "NON_UNIFORMITY_CORRECTION")]
	NonUniformityCorrection,
	#[doc = r#"FPA channel's having this capability support the ability of changing the direction pixels are read out of a FPA. Data from an FPA is generally read out as a line of data. For example, with a line array, the generated pixels can be read out of the array either starting from the left side of the array or the right side of the array.   The ability to set which element is reported first is especially useful in generating images having the correct orientation, i.e. which side of the array is up. This orientation is dependent upon which side of the aircraft the sensor is point at, the left or right side of the plane. Setting this scan direction makes sure the pixels are always returned in the proper orientation regardless of which side of the aircraft the sensor is pointing at."#]
	#[serde(rename = "SCAN_DIRECTION")]
	ScanDirection,
}

#[doc = r#"The enumeration of all reference frames that the gimbal's orientation may be in referenced to."#]
